Governments around the globe are working to digitalize their services.
Bolivia is not an exception.
The Bolivian government has been trying to incentivize the use of modern software systems to let users approach bureaucratic processes in a more efficient way.
However, this is not easy to achieve and requires a major effort from all the institutions that need to create entire software systems for each new procedure.
This project wants to make it easier for different government institutions to digitalize what we commonly call "trámites" here in Bolivia through the reuse of configurable software.
This work was inspired by prior work on the topic and existing systems used to manage workflows in other contexts, while using Petri Nets as a foundation to model the processes.
The implemented prototype is promising. Although it cannot yet support all existing trámites and has some limitations, it provides a solid foundation for future development.

